---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Source from '../components/Source.astro';
import routing from '../../snippets/routing.ts?raw';
import server from '../../snippets/server.ts?raw';
import checkout from '../../snippets/checkout.tsx?raw';
import typeErrors from '../../snippets/type-errors.ts?raw';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repository = 'https://github.com/farisaziz12/tenderlane';
const example = `${repository}/tree/main/examples/react-checkout-multi-psp`;

// The part of type-errors.ts that carries the errors; the imports add nothing here.
const typeErrorsBody = typeErrors.slice(typeErrors.indexOf('createRulesRouter({'));
---

<Layout title="Tenderlane: pick the payment provider for each customer from rules you write once">
  <div class="container home">
    <header class="intro">
      <p class="status">
        Alpha proof of concept. Not published to npm, not production-ready.
        <a href={`${base}/docs/limitations`}>What is missing</a>
      </p>
      <h1>
        Tenderlane picks the payment provider and checkout flow for each customer, from rules you
        write once.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Say you take cards through Stripe in Switzerland and send customers in the US to Polar. With
        Tenderlane you write that down as a short list of rules. Your checkout page reads the rules
        to show the right payment form. Your server reads the same rules before it creates the
        payment, and it sets the price, so the browser never sends one.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        It works with Stripe and Polar today and has React bindings. It ships no UI: the buttons
        and forms are yours.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`${base}/docs/getting-started`}>Run the example</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${base}/docs/architecture`}>Architecture</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repository}>Source</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        As soon as a checkout uses two providers, or two ways of paying with one provider, you
        write the same <code>if (country === ...)</code> three times: where you decide what to
        render, where you handle the Pay button, and in the API route that creates the payment.
        Each provider&rsquo;s SDK has its own objects and its own idea of &ldquo;paid&rdquo;.
        Nothing checks that your three copies agree, and the price often travels from the browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        With Tenderlane you make that decision in one place. The browser and the server both read
        it, and every provider plugs in the same way.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you write</h2>

      <h3>1. Rules: which provider and flow, for which state</h3>
      <p>
        Rules are plain data, and the first one that matches wins. Use <code>hosted</code> to
        send the customer to the provider&rsquo;s own page, or <code>inline</code> to show the
        provider&rsquo;s payment form inside yours.
      </p>
      <Source code={routing} file="docs-site/snippets/routing.ts" />

      <h3>2. One server endpoint, which decides the price</h3>
      <p>
        The browser only says what the customer is buying: <code>{'{ sku, quantity }'}</code>.
        Your server looks up the price, checks the rules again, and only then calls the provider
        with your secret key.
      </p>
      <Source code={server} file="docs-site/snippets/server.ts" />

      <h3>3. One form for every route</h3>
      <p>
        You write the button and the error message. When the customer&rsquo;s country changes,
        the same component switches between a redirect and an inline payment form by itself.
      </p>
      <Source code={checkout} file="docs-site/snippets/checkout.tsx" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the compiler checks</h2>
      <p>
        TypeScript knows which providers you registered, so a rule that names any other one does
        not compile. Neither does a mistyped currency or flow. These are real errors: this file
        only compiles because each one is marked as expected.
      </p>
      <Source code={typeErrorsBody} file="docs-site/snippets/type-errors.ts" />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where it sits</h2>
      <pre class="flow" aria-label="Data flow">{`browser                                         server
───────                                         ──────
app state { country, ... }
   │
router ─► route { provider, flow }
   │
client state machine ── submit() ──────────────► handler.POST
   │                    { provider,              1. drop any client amount, validate
   │                      items: [{ sku, qty }] } 2. derive the server's context
   │                                             3. same router: is this the right provider?
   │                                             4. price items from the catalog
   │                                             5. server adapter ─► PSP API (secret key)
   ◄─────────── { id, status, url | clientSecret } ◄┘
   │
hosted: redirect to url      inline: confirm in the provider's element`}</pre>
      <p>
        Tenderlane is a library that runs in your app and your API route. It is not a service
        and payments do not pass through it. Card details stay in the provider&rsquo;s page or
        iframe; neither you nor Tenderlane sees them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="columns">
      <div>
        <h2>Works today</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Rules router shared by browser and server</li>
          <li>A checkout client with no UI, whose state TypeScript can narrow by status</li>
          <li>React provider, hook and form</li>
          <li>Stripe: Checkout (hosted) and Payment Element (inline)</li>
          <li>Polar: hosted checkout, one product per checkout</li>
          <li>Server-side pricing from a catalog you define, or from Stripe or Polar</li>
          <li>Server-side confirmation: an order hook, a status lookup and verified webhooks</li>
          <li>An adapter API for other providers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Does not exist yet</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Refund, dispute and subscription events; webhook deduplication and storage</li>
          <li>Subscriptions, refunds, saved payment methods</li>
          <li>Failover between providers</li>
          <li>Any provider other than Stripe and Polar</li>
          <li>npm releases of this API</li>
          <li>A run against a live PSP in CI (a Stripe test-mode suite exists and is run by hand)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p>
        You can run the example without a payment account. It uses a built-in demo provider,
        clearly labelled, that moves no money. Add Stripe test keys to switch on the real Stripe
        routes.
      </p>
      <pre class="flow">{`git clone https://github.com/farisaziz12/tenderlane.git
cd tenderlane
pnpm install
pnpm example        # http://localhost:3000`}</pre>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/getting-started`}>Getting started</a> walks through what you see.
        The <a href={example}>example source</a> is under 500 lines.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .home {
    max-width: 820px;
    padding-top: 56px;
    color: var(--text-1);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: 56px;
  }
  .status {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px 10px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-2);
    margin: 0 0 20px;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(26px, 4.2vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 17px;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 0;
  }
  section {
    margin: 0 0 56px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-0);
    margin: 32px 0 6px;
  }
  p {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  a:not(.btn) {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
  code {
    font: 0.88em var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--text-0);
    background: var(--bg-2);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 20px;
  }
  li {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }
  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 32px;
  }
  .flow {
    margin: 16px 0;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    color: var(--text-1);
    font: 12.5px/1.6 var(--font-mono);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
